          %h1#idea Idea
          %p
            Spectral measures are an essential tool of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            on
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+theorem"} spectral theorem
          %p
            In the following, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})
            be the algebra of bounded linear operators on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{H})
            the orthogonal projections.
          %h2#real_spectral_measure real spectral measure
          %p
            The following paragraphs explain the concept of a spectral measure in the real case, sufficient for
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+theorem"} spectral theorem
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/selfadjoint+operator"} selfadjoint operator
          %h3#resolution_of_identity resolution of identity
          %p
            Do not confuse this concept with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+of+unity"} partition of unity
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %p
            definition: A
            %strong resolution of the identity operator
            is a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E: \mathbb{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{H})
            satisfying the following conditions:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                (
                %strong> monotony
                ): For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_1, \lambda_2 \in \mathbb{R}
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2
                we have
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi λ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi λ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\lambda_1) \leq E(\lambda_2)
            %li
              %p
                (
                %strong> continuity from above
                ): for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \in \mathbb{R}
                we have
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mi lim
                          %mrow
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mo →
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mo &gt;
                            %mn 0
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi λ
                        %mo +
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s-\lim_{\epsilon \to 0, \epsilon \gt 0} E(\lambda + \epsilon) = E(\lambda)
            %li
              %p
                (
                %strong> boundary condition
                ):
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi lim
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mo →
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn ∞
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s-\lim_{\epsilon \to -\infty} E(\lambda) = 0
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mi lim
                          %mrow
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mo →
                            %mn ∞
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi 𝟙
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s-\lim_{\epsilon \to \infty} E(\lambda) = \mathbb{1}
          %p
            If there is a finite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \in \mathbb{R}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi λ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{\lambda} = 0
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \leq \mu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi λ
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝟙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{\lambda} = \mathbb{1}
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \geq \mu
            = succeed "," do
              %strong bounded
            = succeed "." do
              %strong unbounded
          %h3#spectral_measure_and_spectral_integral spectral measure and spectral integral
          %p
            Let E be a spectral resolution and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            be a bounded interval in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            with respect to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mtext for
                            %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                            %mi I
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mtext for
                            %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                            %mi I
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mtext for
                            %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                            %mi I
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mtext for
                            %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                            %mi I
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E(J):= \begin{cases}
                  
                  E(y-) - E(x)  &amp; \text{for }\quad I=(x,y) \\
                  E(y-) - E(x-) &amp; \text{for }\quad I=[x,y) \\
                  E(y)  - E(x)  &amp; \text{for }\quad I=(x,y] \\
                  E(y)  - E(x-) &amp; \text{for }\quad I=[x,y] \\
                  
                  \\end{cases}
          %p
            This allows us to define the integral of a step function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \alpha_k \chi_{I_k}
            with respect to E as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi k
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\integral u(\lambda) dE(\lambda) := \sum_{k=1}^{n} \alpha_k E(I_k)
          %p The value of this integral is a bounded operator.
          %p As in conventional measure and integration theory, the integral can be extended from step functions to Borel-measurable functions. In this case one often used notation is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E(u) = \integral u(\lambda) dE(\lambda)
          %p
            For general function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, E(u)
            need not be a bounded operator of course, the domain of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(u)
            is (theorem):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo :
                  %mo ∫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 2
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D(E(u)) = \{ f \in \mathcal{H} : \int |u(\lambda)|^2 d\langle E(\lambda)f, f\rangle \lt \infty \}
          %h1#spectrum_of_representations_of_groups_the_snag_theorem Spectrum of Representations of Groups, the SNAG Theorem
          %p
            The SNAG theorem is necessary to explain the spectrum condition of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            be a locally compact, abelian topological group,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \mathcal{G}
            the character group of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            a Hilbert space and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            an unitary representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            in the algebra of bounded operators of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            %strong SNAG
            theorem (SNAG = Stone-Naimark-Ambrose-Godement):
          #specmeasure.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                %strong> Theorem
                \: There is a unique regular spectral measure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \mathcal{G}
                such that:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi χ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{U}(g) = \int_{\chi\in\hat \mathcal{G}} \langle g, \chi\rangle \mathcal{P}(d\chi) \qquad \forall g \in \mathcal{G}
          %p
            The equality holds in the weak sense, i.e. the integral converges in the weak operator topology. The
            %strong spectrum
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{G})
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi spec
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} spec\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{G})
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
          %h2#the_case_of_the_translation_group The Case of the Translation Group
          %p
            The groups of translations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R^n
            is both isomorph to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R^n
            and to it’s own character group, every character is of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \mapsto exp(i \langle a, k\rangle)
            for a fixed
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \R^n
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#specmeasure"}
            becomes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                        %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{U}(t) = \int_{k\in \R^n} e^{i \langle t, k\rangle} \mathcal{P}(k) \qquad \forall t \in \mathcal{T}
          %p
            This allows us to talk about the support of the spectral measure, i.e. the spectrum of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{T})
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R^n
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+measure"} projection measure
          %h2#references References
          %p
